No build directory exists, and a full ClickHouse build is prohibitively expensive to run here. The change is minimal and follows the exact idiom already used in `src/Interpreters/sortBlock.cpp` (`compareAtWithCollation(..., *description.collator)`), so no new include is needed — `Collator` is forward-declared in `IColumn.h` and dereferencing the `shared_ptr` to bind a `const Collator &` reference doesn't require the complete type.

## Summary

I implemented the collation-aware fill-group detection fix in `src/Processors/Transforms/FillingTransform.cpp`.

**What was wrong:** During WITH FILL group detection (when `use_with_fill_by_sorting_prefix` is enabled), sort-prefix equality was tested with `IColumn::compareAt`, which compares strings byte-for-byte. So collation-equal spellings like `'a'` and `'A'` (under a case-insensitive collation) were treated as *different* sorting prefixes and each got filled separately across the whole domain — producing spurious rows.

**The fix:** I added a small helper that consults the collation attached to each sort-description entry:

```cpp
static int compareSortPrefixAt(
    const IColumn & lhs, size_t lhs_row,
    const IColumn & rhs, size_t rhs_row,
    const SortColumnDescription & desc)
{
    if (desc.collator)
        return lhs.compareAtWithCollation(lhs_row, rhs_row, rhs, desc.nulls_direction, *desc.collator);

    return lhs.compareAt(lhs_row, rhs_row, rhs, desc.nulls_direction);
}
```

Then I routed both sort-prefix comparison sites through it:

1. The cross-chunk check that decides whether the first row of a new chunk continues the previous chunk's sorting prefix (`transform`).
2. The `getRangeEnd` predicate that delimits a sorting-prefix range within a chunk — this is the single predicate used by *both* the linear-probe path (runs < 16) and the binary-search path (runs > 16), so both paths automatically stay consistent.

Because the grouping logic itself is unchanged and only the equality comparison became collation-aware, the fix naturally satisfies all the stated requirements: collation-equal values form one fill group (the 6-row `a/A/b` case), long/short runs group identically, boundaries track correctly across blocks (no spurious rows for the 40k interleaved case), and it works for both ASC and DESC since `compareAtWithCollation` respects the direction the same way `compareAt` did.

The `use_with_fill_by_sorting_prefix` setting already exists in `src/Core/Settings.cpp` (default `true`), so no settings change was needed. No files under `tests/` were touched.

